WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:06.859
This scene from season 3 of The CW’s hit
show The 100 unexpectedly set off a firestorm

00:00:06.859 --> 00:00:10.519
of anger and shock from it’s fans this year.

00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:13.929
The episode called “Thirteen” premiered
on March 3rd.

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:16.970
Right where this google trend chart for the
show peaks.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.060
So, what happened?

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.470
Well, these two characters, Clarke and Lexa,
were in love.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:25.330
And after seasons of pining for each other

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:31.400
Caroline: Finally get together, have a happy
moment, Lexa walks out of the bedroom and

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.410
gets shot by a stray bullet meant for Clarke.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:38.460
Fans were deeply deeply hurt on a level that
the writers did not anticipate

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.300
So, a character died, and that seems to happen
all the time these days.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.860
What’s the big deal?

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:52.150
Well, the scene perpetuates a pretty toxic
trope, called “Bury your Gays” where LGBTQ

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.960
characters just seem to never have happy endings.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.940
Caroline: The thing is it didn’t start with
television.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.239
To understand what’s happening here you
have to go back to lesbian pulp novels that

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:07.049
sprung up in the 1950s and 60s.

00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:11.789
The genre was notorious for building up a
relationship between two women and then at

00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:14.880
the very end they would be punished in some
way.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:22.670
Caroline: Marijane Meeker was one of the most
famous Lesbian Pulp novelists of the time

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:26.790
She wrote under a few pseudonyms including
Vin Packer for Gold Medal.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:27.790
Caroline: And her first novel Spring Fire
followed that to a tee

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:33.049
Meeker was told by her editor from the beginning
that the book should not have a happy ending

00:01:33.049 --> 00:01:36.659
or the post office might seize it as obscene.

00:01:36.659 --> 00:01:40.929
In the end, one woman goes crazy and the other
renounces her lesbianism altogether.

00:01:40.929 --> 00:01:46.079
Caroline: This is why when Patricia Highsmith
wrote The Price of Salt, which the Movie Carol

00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:56.250
was based on, it was immediately a big deal
simply because it had a happy ending.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:04.159
Caroline: If you look at the history of queer
women dying on television it goes back to

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:05.299
even the 70s.

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:10.060
Autostraddle has a pretty comprehensive list
of queer deaths on TV.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.980
One of the first deaths comes from the show
Executive Suite where the character Julie

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:16.980
died

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.680
Caroline: Her love interest had just walked
into traffic after realizing her lesbianism

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.280
and Julie was chasing her.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.739
Autostraddle currently lists 152 queer deaths
since 1976

00:02:25.739 --> 00:02:29.549
Caroline: One of the main ones that people
always point to is Tara on Buffy which actually

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:34.200
The 100 death really eerily mirrored.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:40.700
Caroline: The same week that Lexa died on
The 100 a character on The Magicians was introduced

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.120
as a gay woman of color

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.209
And then killed herself two scenes later

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:49.909
Caroline: Also that week Denise on the Walking
Dead got an arrow in the eye when in the comics

00:02:49.909 --> 00:02:51.750
that happened to a guy.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:56.610
Caroline: On Vampire Diaries a gay witch couple
decided to kill themselves together rather

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:57.769
than one of them just dying.

00:02:57.769 --> 00:02:59.930
So they both died.

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:05.220
On Empire, Camila killed her wife, Mimi with
poison and then was killed herself.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.040
All of these deaths on their own might not
seem that crazy.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.409
But let’s put this in perspective.

00:03:10.409 --> 00:03:17.030
During the 2015-2016 tv season, 22 lesbian
characters that appeared in 3 episodes or

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.640
more were killed off.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.500
That’s 25% of all female deaths on TV and
10% of all deaths on TV.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.830
Caroline: Proportionally speaking that makes
no sense.

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:29.170
So, why did lexa’s death ignite such a visceral
reaction?

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:34.780
Caroline: These fans being able to talk to
the creators in a way that they’ve never

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.922
been able to really accelerated a lot of this
conversation.

00:03:36.922 --> 00:03:43.810
The creator of The 100, Jason Rothenberg,
later apologized to fans in an open letter,

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:49.269
acknowledging that in trying to heighten the
drama, he ended up perpetuating a disturbing

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:50.269
trope.

00:03:50.269 --> 00:03:54.370
Caroline: I would never suggest that a TV
show could never kill off a gay character

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.790
or that anything is forbidden from writers.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.489
But I do think it’s worth being conscious
of this trope and knowing what they’re doing

00:04:00.489 --> 00:04:01.489
when they are doing it.

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:04.749
And making sure that the death has some resonance
and really that’s what we should be doing

00:04:04.749 --> 00:04:07.580
anyway is making sure that a character death
means something.

